# For a fresh clone you don't need to copy this file — the committed
# .env.<mode> files provide sensible defaults. To override them locally
# (e.g. point dev at a real backend), copy this file to `.env.local` —
# NOT `.env`. `.env` is the LOWEST-priority Vite source and is shadowed
# by every committed mode file; `.env.local` beats them all.
# Environment variables are only read at Vite startup; restart after edits.
#
# Three committed environments (no secrets — safe to commit):
#   .env.development  npm run dev        — mocks on (VITE_ENABLE_MSW=true)
#   .env.qa           npm run build:qa   — QA build, mocks bundled + booted
#   .env.production   npm run build      — real prod; VITE_API_BASE_URL is
#                                          commented so the env gate FAILS
#                                          unless you set it here or via
#                                          the host build environment.
#
# Vite precedence (highest to lowest, applied per variable):
#   process env > .env.<mode>.local > .env.local > .env.<mode> (committed) > .env
# `.env` and `.env.local` are gitignored (H5); the three mode files are not.
# NOTE: `.env` LOSES to every committed mode file — use `.env.local` for
# personal overrides.
#
# ── Overriding a committed mode file at deploy time ──
# The committed mode files are DEFAULTS, not locks. Real environment
# variables win, so you can flip mocks or repoint the API without editing
# any committed file:
#   - Hosting dashboards (Vercel/Netlify/Amplify/…): set VITE_ENABLE_MSW
#     and/or VITE_API_BASE_URL in the project's build-env settings.
#   - Command line (bash/BusyBox):
#       VITE_ENABLE_MSW=false npm run build:qa
#       VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com npm run build
#   - Command line (PowerShell):
#       $env:VITE_ENABLE_MSW='false'; npm run build:qa
# When turning MSW off you MUST also supply VITE_API_BASE_URL — the env
# gate refuses to build a mock-less artifact without a backend URL.

# The three modes below are LOCAL-OVERRIDE recipes: copy the block you
# want into `.env.local` (gitignored) — not `.env`, which is shadowed by
# the committed `.env.<mode>` files.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mode (a) — DEFAULT: MSW mock backend (no network, no backend needed)
#
# When VITE_ENABLE_MSW=true the base URL may be omitted entirely and is
# ignored if present — mock handlers always match same-origin relative
# paths regardless of what VITE_API_BASE_URL contains.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
VITE_ENABLE_MSW=true

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mode (b) — Dev-server proxy to the LIVE backend (bypasses broken CORS)
#
# The Vite dev server proxies configured path prefixes (/user, /register,
# /GetAll) to VITE_DEV_PROXY_TARGET. The browser stays same-origin so no
# preflight is needed. Session cookies are rewritten to `localhost`.
#
# The AWS API Gateway stage path (`/prod`) belongs in the TARGET value —
# http-proxy prepends target-path to request-path (e.g. /user/login →
# https://<host>/prod/user/login upstream). Do not add path rewriting.
#
# The dev-server port is irrelevant in this mode (browser talks to the
# dev server directly; upstream sees only rewritten headers).
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=
#   VITE_ENABLE_MSW=false
#   VITE_DEV_PROXY_TARGET=https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com/prod

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mode (c) — Direct absolute URL (production-style; requires working CORS)
#
# The browser calls the backend directly. Currently NOT viable for the
# live AWS API Gateway because its CORS is broken (missing OPTIONS
# preflight, quoted literal values in Access-Control-Allow-Headers).
# Use Mode (b) for live-backend development instead.
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com/prod
#   VITE_ENABLE_MSW=false
